# Data plane (RAG, files, sharing)

Architecture reference: `../docs/rag-vector-architecture.md`. Confirm table names and dimensions in repo before generating migrations.

## Retrieval philosophy

**Scope is part of the query.** Every search runs inside an explicit scope value object (global / project / topic / linked sets)—not “search everything, filter in Python.”

**Filter where data lives.** Predicates belong in SQL against pgvector rows. Post-filtering embeddings is a correctness and privacy failure mode.

**Metadata carries placement.** Chunks and documents record who, what project, what topic, what folder, and visibility—extend via migrations and backfill when the product adds axes; don’t bolt scope on later in application code alone.

**Navigation context is input.** Whatever the user sees (project, topic, folder) must be serialized on the request; log resolved scope when debugging retrieval.

## Registry and ingestion

Register projects and files in `rag.*` before writing chunks; IDs mirror app UUIDs. Ingestion is asynchronous—upload marks pending; worker embeds through the gateway into the canonical chunk table (prefer the indexed, production dimension table).

Dual-store schemas in the app DB are legacy unless the task explicitly migrates them.

## Sharing and linked context

Cross-user access is enforced at query time—revocation means “exclude on next read,” not necessarily re-embedding everything. Cap fan-out for linked projects; bias ranking toward the user’s home scope when combining corpora.

User-facing compartments are **folders**; object-store buckets are infrastructure naming—keep user language consistent in APIs.

## Gateway dependency

Embeddings and chat models come from the shared LiteLLM stack. Don’t stand up parallel vector stores (e.g. Chroma) without an explicit architectural decision.

## Config

All knobs through `Settings` and `.env.example`—no scattered environment reads in services.
